{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789820797,"nonce":"sHpPndFK7g6HlESZu9E3bNOT5cQ=","title":"Interpreter: a function with more than 65535 values fails with no fallback","message":"`Reg = u16` in the bytecode interpreter, one register per SSA value, and\na function past that panics with \"register overflow\" (now an\nUnsupportedInstruction error) rather than running. A module body of a\nfew hundred lines reached 66k values before the value sweep landed\n(1a47c0f); the sweep brought it under, but nothing stops a larger\nprogram.\n\nWhat the interpreter cannot run, native code runs, when there is native\ncode (`run_natively_or`), but the entry it asks for is one already\ncompiled; a cold function too large for the interpreter should be\ncompiled at tier 0 through `lazy_compile` and called natively, the way\nan instruction it cannot execute already falls back.","files":null}]}